688:
(a) Eligibility. In accordance with O.C.G.A. § 36-12-5, whenever any person shall die in Whitfield County under circumstances whereby the decedent, his or her family, or next-of-kin are indigent and unable to provide for the decent interment of the decedent, the Whitfield County Board of Commissioners shall reimburse such person or entity as may have incurred such cost a reasonable sum, the exact amount of which it shall solely determine, for such expense or portion thereof. The Whitfield County Administrator, or his or her designee, shall be authorized to determine eligibility based upon such facts as he or she may require. An affidavit, substantially similar in form to a Pauper's Affidavit, shall constitute adequate evidence, but shall not be required in every instance, in the discretion of the County Administrator.
(b) Services included. A decent interment shall include the following minimum services performed by or at the direction of a licensed funeral director:
(1) Transportation of decedent from place of death (or Georgia Crime Laboratory, if applicable) to funeral home;
(2) In lieu of traditional burial, cremation at a licensed cremation facility shall be accomplished, unless the next-of-kin or personal representative shall refuse to execute a cremation authorization or unless no next-of-kin shall be located.
(3) Cremation at a licensed facility and return of the cremated remains to the next-of-kin or personal representative shall fully satisfy all requirements hereof.
(4) Should the next-of-kin or personal representative refuse to authorize cremation based upon a sincerely held religious objection, then care procedures for decedent, including cleaning, dressing, and casketing shall be provided.
(5) Provision of casket, made of either wood, metal, or sturdy cardboard;
(6) Cloth covering for casket or U.S. Flag, if decedent is known to be a Veteran;
(7) Hearse transportation to pauper section of West Hill Cemetery or V.A. National Cemetery.
(c) Services not included. A decent interment shall not necessarily include the following services and such shall not be required nor paid for by Whitfield County:
(1) Embalming services;
(2) Use of family or other cemetery for which additional cost would be incurred;
(3) Graveside services performed by funeral director or staff;
(4) Use of tent or chairs;
(5) Radio and/or newspaper notices;
(6) Provision of certified copy of death certificate;
(d) Reimbursement of costs to providers. Whitfield County shall reimburse actual expenses incurred, up to a maximum of $800.00 per indigent decedent who shall have been cremated and shall have been determined eligible by the Whitfield County Administrator prior to the provision of any services by a licensed funeral director. Whitfield County shall reimburse actual expenses incurred, up to a maximum of $2,100.00 per indigent decedent who shall have been buried and shall have been determined eligible by the Whitfield County Administrator prior to the provision of any services by a licensed funeral director.
(e) Reimbursement of county for amounts paid. To the extent Whitfield County, in the discretion of the County Administrator or his or her designee, shall determine that any person for whom it has expended any sum pursuant to this section is not or was not indigent as a result of owning sufficient real or personal property, then Whitfield County shall be permitted to seek reimbursement of some or all amounts expended, whether through the decedent's estate or other lawful means.
(Ord. of 8-8-05; Ord. No. 2015-12151, §§ 1, 2, 12-14-15; Ord. No. 2023-1009, §§ 1, 2, 10-9-23)
State Law reference— Burial, appropriation for, O.C.G.A., § 36-12-5 et seq.

715:
(a) A solicitor, prior to soliciting goods or services of any kind, including giving away any goods or services in person at residences and places of business in the unincorporated area of the county without the consent or invitation of the occupant shall:
(1) Be at least 18 years of age at the time of the application;
(2) Be a citizen of the United States or lawfully present in the United States;
(3) File an application for and receive a permit from the County Clerk;
(b) Persons requesting a permit for soliciting door-to-door in private residential areas shall be required to produce information for each vehicle(s) being used, including two photos of each vehicle (front/side view and rear/side view,) along with photographs of the vehicle license plate. Such information shall be provided to the Whitfield County Sheriff's Office.
(c) Any person working as an employee of a business shall demonstrate proof of employment from the owner of the business along with written permission from the owner to apply for a permit. Proof of employment may constitute two current paycheck stubs or evidence of an employment agreement.
(d) An independent contractor (persons independently employed or receiving 1099 forms instead of a W-2 form) must apply for separate permits personally.
(e) All applications for a permit must include, at a minimum, the following information and shall be signed by the applicant:
(1) Name and address of the applicant;
(2) Date of Birth;
(3) Social Security Number;
(4) Physical description;
(5) Name, address and telephone number of the applicant's employer;
(6) Name, address and telephone number of the applicant's supervisor;
(7) Brief description of goods or services to be sold;
(8) Description of motor vehicle(s) to be used by the applicant in conducting the canvassing or solicitation;
(9) Release to obtain a criminal history; and
(10) Photocopy of driver's license or passport of the applicant.
(Ord. No. 2025-1110, § 1, 11-10-25)

721:
